    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d691">

                <pb facs="1119" n="983" sameAs="frus:frus1888p1/pg983"/>

                <head>No. 689.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Bayard to Mr. Thompson.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1888-11-30"><hi rendition="#italic">November</hi> 30,
                        1888</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 137.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I transmit herewith for your information
                    add guidaoce, copy of a note<anchor n="*" xml:id="d691-f1"/> which I have
                    addressed to Mr. Stephen Preston the representative oi Hayti in this eountiy,
                    setting foith the confederations which make it impossible for this Government to
                    accept as valid the seizure and condemnation of the steamer <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Haytian Republic.</hi></p>
                <p>The examination of the very full and intelligent report received from Captain
                    Bamsey, of the U. S. S. <hi rendition="#italic">Boston</hi>, with th-e papers
                    and correspondence therewith submitted, left no ground for doubt as to the
                    entire justice of the positive conclusion of this Government in the premises,
                    and as the administrative authorities now in power at Port au Prince appear to
                    have deferred the examination and decision of the &amp;ase of the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Haytian Republic</hi> to the Govefximeat of the Dnitol
                    States Mr. Preston’s powers will Sio doubt suffice to enable the question to be
                    disposed of without delay.</p>
                <p>While you will afoid any renewal of discussion at Port aa Prince, and distinctly
                    decline it should it be proposed, you will not fail to impress upon those
                    assuming the responsibility of speaking for Hayti in the conduct of her
                    relations to the United States that this Government has considered /t to be
                    sufficient to convey its decision of the case of the <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Haytian Republic</hi> in order to have it promptly accepted and voluntarily
                    acted upon by Hayti.</p>
                <p>It is necessary, therefore that you should receive from the Haytian authorities
                    assurances of the immediate replacement on the steamer <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Haytian Republic</hi> of those portions of her machinery which it is
                    understood were removed under their orders, so that she may be restored to her
                    usual sea-going capacity and fitness for maritime service.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">T. F. Bayard.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
